quiz:
  - q: 'Two desks each report their year. Desk A grossed 12 million with 7 million of allocated costs; Desk B grossed 8 million with 2 million of costs. Which is the better business?'
    opts: ['Desk A, because it grossed more', 'Desk B: it keeps 6 million against Desk A''s 5 million, and spends far less to earn each pound', 'They are the same, because both made a profit']
    right: 1
    why: 'Gross P&amp;L hides the costs. Net is \(12 - 7 = 5\) for A and \(8 - 2 = 6\) for B, and B''s costs are \(25\%\) of gross against A''s \(58\%\). That''s exactly the comparison P&amp;L attribution exists to make.'
  - q: 'Why does the risk team set and monitor a desk''s limits independently, with authority that doesn''t pass through the desk''s own managers?'
    opts: ['So that nobody whose positions are being limited can loosen or overrule the limits, especially under pressure', 'Because traders don''t understand risk', 'To slow the desk down so it trades less']
    right: 0
    why: 'A limit the desk can bend is no limit. Independence means the people constrained can''t change the constraint on a bad day. Several famous losses happened when this broke down, for example when one person controlled both the trading and the records of it.'
  - q: 'A firm holds 4,700 contracts against a 5,000-contract position limit and a strategy tries to buy 500 more. What should happen, and where?'
    opts: ['The order is sent, and compliance reviews it at the end of the day', 'The exchange rejects the whole order', 'A pre-trade check in the firm''s own code caps the order at 300 before any exchange sees it']
    right: 2
    why: '\(4{,}700 + 500 = 5{,}200 > 5{,}000\), so only \(300\) fit. At machine speeds a rule enforced after the fact is a rule broken first, so the check runs in software, before the order leaves the building.'

